#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use modern tools to implement methods for error suppression, mitigation, and"
" correction. We focus on the directed execution model using Samplomatic and "
"addons."
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Modern workflows"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Modern workflows {#modern-workflows}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This course has covered a wide variety of methods for handling quantum noise"
" and errors. We intentionally avoided some black-box solutions so that we "
"could see how these methods work. Now we are ready to use these methods in "
"their most modern form. Specifically, we explore some highly optimized "
"error-handling tools that hide some of the complexity from us. We also "
"explore Samplomatic and addons such as shaded lightcones (SLC), which give "
"us very fine control over certain mitigation processes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this video, Dr. Chris Porter gives an overview of these modern tools. We "
"plan to add example workflows to this lesson at a later date. However, this "
"lesson already contains all the information that you need to use these tools"
" and to pass the badging exam.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<IBMVideo id=\"135107080\" title=\"In this video, Chris Porter provides an "
"overview of modern tools for handling errors in quantum computation.\"/>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overview {#overview}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Early IBM Quantum primitives, such as Estimator and Sampler, exposed error "
"mitigation through a small number of preset levels. Later releases provided "
"more direct control over individual techniques, including choices of zero-"
"noise extrapolation strategy, Pauli twirling configurations, and "
"randomization parameters. However, these settings remained largely global, "
"and applied uniformly across an entire circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A diagram showing that different phases of execution models have increased"
" user control.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/modern-"
"workflows/phases.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As quantum workloads become more sophisticated, developers increasingly need"
" the ability to tailor execution and mitigation strategies to specific "
"regions of a circuit. The following diagram outlines several key tools that "
"this lesson discusses, and shows where they fit within a typical workflow.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A diagram showing that workflows typically progress from mapping problems "
"to quantum circuits, to transpilation and optimization, to execution and "
"post-processing. Each of these steps has modern tools associated with "
"it.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/modern-"
"workflows/workflow.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The directed execution model addresses the need for modular control over "
"execution, and it integrates many of these tools.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Directed execution model {#directed-execution-model}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rather than making implicit execution decisions, the directed execution "
"model follows user-provided directives. This approach improves transparency "
"and makes complex workflows easier to inspect and debug. Some key features "
"of this model include:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Per-layer configuration through labeled circuit regions\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Identification of unique and repeated layers for efficient noise learning\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Batched execution workflows\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Structured post-processing\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Close integration with addons such as shaded lightcones\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The model is built around two key components:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Samplomatic, which defines execution recipes\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Executor, which carries out those recipes and returns samples\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Most workflows that use Executor can also be implemented with Sampler, "
"because both ultimately produce samples. The key advantage of Executor is "
"efficiency. Tasks such as generating twirling configurations are often much "
"faster in the server-side near-time environment than on the client. "
"Estimator and Sampler remain supported, and might still be the most "
"convenient choice for many workloads.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Before execution, however, you must construct and prepare a circuit. Qiskit "
"provides several addons that help with circuit generation, simulation, and "
"optimization. For more information about any of the following tools, follow "
"the links.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Build quantum circuits {#build-quantum-circuits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For some applications or new approaches, users must determine how to map "
"their problem to a quantum circuit. In many domains, however, Qiskit addons "
"can automate important parts of this process.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Optimization mapper {#optimization-mapper}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Optimization mapper](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-addon-opt-mapper/) "
"helps express classical optimization problems in forms that are suitable for"
" quantum algorithms. Key functionality includes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Construct objective functions and constraints\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Convert problems into QUBO formulations\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Generate Hamiltonian or operator representations\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![An image of a molecule and graph being mapped to "
"qubits.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/modern-"
"workflows/mappers.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Fermionic mapper {#fermionic-mapper}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Fermionic mapper](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-fermions) "
"converts fermionic operators into qubit operators that are suitable for "
"quantum hardware. It supports mappings such as Jordan-Wigner, which converts"
" operators on indistinguishable fermions to Pauli operators on "
"distinguishable qubits, while preserving fermionic statistics. This addon is"
" useful for quantum chemistry and materials science applications.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The previous two addons focus on the initial mapping of a problem structure "
"to a quantum circuit. It is equally important, however, to efficiently map "
"the time evolution of a process onto a quantum circuit, and to optimize a "
"circuit by reducing its depth. The next few addons address these tasks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Multi-product formulas (MPF) {#multi-product-formulas-mpf}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Multi-product formulas](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-addon-mpf/) improve"
" the accuracy of quantum time-evolution simulations by combining multiple "
"approximations. Specifically, you create circuits with varying numbers of "
"Trotter steps, so that you can use a weighted combination of the circuit "
"results. For a given target accuracy, MPF can often use shallower circuits "
"than standard Trotter methods. The cost is that you must run multiple "
"circuits and combine their results classically.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![An image showing Trotter circuits with different numbers of "
"steps.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/modern-"
"workflows/mpf.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "AQC-Tensor {#aqc-tensor}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[AQC-Tensor](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-addon-aqc-tensor/) performs "
"approximate quantum compilation using tensor-network methods. A common use "
"case is to replace a portion of a deep circuit with a shallower variational "
"approximation. The resulting circuit can achieve similar accuracy with "
"significantly less depth.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![An image showing the first few layers of a quantum circuit being "
"compressed using AQC.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-"
"advantage/modern-workflows/aqc.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Transpilation {#transpilation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You have already used transpilation many times in this course. You know that"
" after an abstract circuit is constructed, it must be prepared for execution"
" on hardware. However, we have not yet explored the multiple stages of "
"transpilation in detail.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Key transpilation stages include:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Layout: map logical qubits to physical qubits\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Translation: rewrite operations using the backend's native gate set\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Routing: insert and optimize SWAP operations required by hardware "
"connectivity\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"These processes are interrelated. For example, the layout should minimize "
"depth even after routing is taken into account. The layout should also avoid"
" the most error-prone qubits and connections. These stages are often "
"automated, but advanced users might choose to specify custom layouts or "
"other constraints when needed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![An image showing how the qubits in an abstract quantum circuit need to be "
"assigned to real physical qubits on a quantum computer as part of "
"transpilation.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/modern-"
"workflows/transp-mapping.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To learn more about transpilation, see the [Introduction to "
"transpilation](/guides/transpile) guide.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Look at the following quantum circuit, and assume that you need to run it on"
" a quantum computer with a heavy-hex layout, part of which is shown. (a) "
"Propose a mapping from the qubit numbers in the abstract circuit to the "
"qubit numbers in the physical quantum computer that minimizes the number of "
"SWAP gates required. (b) Is your choice unique, or might there be other "
"equally good choices? (c) Even if your choice is the sole optimal layout for"
" reducing SWAP gates, what are some reasons that the transpiler might choose"
" a different mapping?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A two-part image. The right shows a quantum circuit with six qubits and "
"five CNOT gates. The left shows the heavy-hex topology of a region of a real"
" quantum computer.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-"
"advantage/modern-workflows/check-in-transp.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(a) There could be many correct answers, but note that only abstract qubit 0"
" has entangling (two-qubit) gates with three neighbors. All others "
"participate in two-qubit operations with only two neighbors. This circuit "
"can be mapped to a heavy-hex topology without any SWAP gates. Any layout "
"that achieves this is acceptable. Using $\\text{aq[0]}$ to refer to abstract"
" qubit zero, and $\\text{tq[0]}$ for transpiled qubit zero, one such layout "
"is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\text{aq[0]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[15],}\\\\\n"
"\\text{aq[1]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[16],}\\\\\n"
"\\text{aq[2]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[19],}\\\\\n"
"\\text{aq[3]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[14],}\\\\\n"
"\\text{aq[4]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[17],}\\\\\n"
"\\text{aq[5]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[25]}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(b) This layout is not unique. Many other layouts avoid SWAP gates "
"altogether. Another example is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\text{aq[0]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[3],}\\\\\n"
"\\text{aq[1]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[4],}\\\\\n"
"\\text{aq[2]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[8],}\\\\\n"
"\\text{aq[3]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[2],}\\\\\n"
"\\text{aq[4]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[5],}\\\\\n"
"\\text{aq[5]} & \\rightarrow \\text{tq[13]}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(c) Sometimes a specific qubit is more error-prone than most others on the "
"chip. Also, some connections between qubits are more error-prone than "
"others. The transpiler attempts to find a layout that avoids unnecessary "
"SWAP gates, but that also uses the highest-fidelity qubits and connections "
"available on the selected quantum computer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Reduce the effects of noise and errors {#reduce-the-effects-of-noise-and-"
"errors}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Several Qiskit addons focus specifically on reducing the impact of noise and"
" hardware imperfections.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Operator backpropagation and noise-aware circuit transformations {#operator-"
"backpropagation-and-noise-aware-circuit-transformations}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We have already discussed operator propagation in the context of coherent "
"Pauli checks in error correction. However, operator propagation is useful "
"more generally. There are two main ways to use the propagation of an "
"operator through a quantum circuit: (1) absorb the last few layers of a "
"quantum circuit into the observables being measured, and (2) take into "
"account the likely effects of errors in the circuit, and compensate for them"
" by measuring a different observable. The next two tools use these "
"approaches.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Operator backpropagation {#operator-backpropagation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Operator backpropagation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-addon-"
"obp) reduces circuit depth by absorbing gates from the end of a circuit into"
" the measured observable. For example, consider the following circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![An image showing a quantum circuit with several entangling and rotation "
"layers, followed by a ladder of CNOTs from qubit 0 down to qubit 5. Finally,"
" a Z measurement is made on qubit 5.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-"
"quantum-advantage/modern-workflows/backprop.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This circuit contains rotation and entangling layers that appear nontrivial,"
" followed by a ladder of CNOT gates and a measurement of `Z` on qubit 5. "
"This circuit has a two-qubit depth of 7. This depth is before transpilation."
" In practice, you typically apply backpropagation after transpilation, but "
"this example is for teaching purposes only. Recall that there are rules for "
"propagating an observable past a gate. In particular, when a CNOT gate has "
"target $q_t$ and control $q_c$, a $Z_t$ observable on $q_t$ becomes $Z_c "
"Z_t$ when propagated backward past the CNOT gate: $Z_t \\rightarrow Z_c "
"Z_t$. In this example circuit, if you propagate the observable $Z_5$ back "
"through the entire ladder of CNOT gates, you get the observable $Z_5 Z_4 Z_3"
" Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$, or in standard Qiskit notation, simply $ZZZZZZ$. If we "
"measure this observable instead of only $Z_5$, we can remove the entire "
"ladder of CNOT gates, which reduces the two-qubit depth from 7 to 2. This "
"observable still requires a measurement in only one basis on each qubit, so "
"it does not increase the number of shots that we require. This is not always"
" the case. Generally, more complicated observables require more shots, and "
"sometimes more measurements in different bases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The overall strategy of operator backpropagation includes the following "
"steps:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shorten the executed circuit\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Replace the original observable with an expanded observable\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Trade circuit depth for increased sampling requirements\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The previous circuit is an obvious example in which backpropagation is "
"advantageous. In general, however, it is not easy to identify the circuits "
"that benefit most from backpropagation. To decide when to use "
"backpropagation, check how much depth you can remove from your circuit, and "
"weigh that reduction against the increased sampling requirements.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Propagated noise absorption (PNA) {#propagated-noise-absorption-pna}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Propagated noise absorption](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-"
"addon-pna) is structurally similar to operator backpropagation. However, "
"instead of propagating an operator back through the ideal gates in a "
"circuit, PNA incorporates a learned noise model and propagates the effects "
"of noise directly into the observable. PNA does the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Learns noise behavior from the hardware\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Propagates the noise model through the circuit\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Produces a modified observable that compensates for gate noise\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![An image showing the gate errors on many layers of a quantum circuit being"
" propagated forward to the final observable "
"measurement.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/modern-"
"workflows/pna.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When the modified observable is measured on noisy hardware, the resulting "
"expectation values better approximate the ideal noiseless result. PNA has "
"two types of overhead. First, you must learn the noise model, which requires"
" running additional circuits. Second, PNA can produce more complicated "
"observables, which can increase sampling requirements.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Circuit cutting {#circuit-cutting}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Circuit cutting](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-addon-cutting/) partitions"
" a large circuit into smaller subcircuits that can run independently. You "
"then make enough measurements to recombine the results. Circuit cutting does"
" the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reduces hardware requirements\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Allows computations larger than available qubit counts (wider circuits)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Helps address depth and connectivity limitations (deeper circuits)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reconstructs the original result through classical post-processing\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![An image showing two circuits being cut, so that they can be run "
"separately and recombined later in post-"
"processing.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/modern-"
"workflows/cutting.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Samplomatic {#samplomatic}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Many of the techniques in the previous sections complement other suppression"
" and mitigation methods covered throughout this course, including:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dynamical decoupling\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli twirling\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "M3\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "TREX\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Probabilistic error cancellation (PEC)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In many workflows, you can apply different techniques to different regions "
"of the same circuit. As we have seen, many of these techniques also come "
"with quantum overhead. We therefore sometimes need fine, detailed control "
"over noise learning, sampling, and the application of twirling sequences. "
"These are some of the motivations behind Samplomatic.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Samplomatic](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/samplomatic) is an open-"
"source library that enables customized sampling, randomization, and error-"
"mitigation workflows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Its first task is to analyze a circuit and divide it into labeled regions, "
"or boxes. These boxes serve two purposes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "They visually partition the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "They create indexed regions that can be configured independently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Users can attach annotations that describe how each box should be treated "
"during execution. Examples include:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Applying Pauli twirling to all boxes\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Applying twirling only to selected boxes\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Using different twirling groups in different regions\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Injecting learned noise into specific layers\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Customizing mitigation strategies at a fine-grained level\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Samplomatic also identifies repeated boxed regions. Because identical layers"
" typically exhibit similar noise characteristics, you only need to perform "
"noise learning once for each unique layer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Samplomatic outputs {#samplomatic-outputs}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Samplomatic produces two primary objects:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Template circuit\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Samplex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The template circuit is a boxed, transpiled, parameterized circuit that is "
"ready for execution. The samplex contains all the information that is "
"required to generate and process circuit variants. Samplex contents "
"include:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Parameter values for parameterized circuits\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Randomization selections\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sampling distributions\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Post-processing information\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Additional execution metadata\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Formally, a samplex is a probability distribution over the parameters of a "
"template circuit, together with auxiliary information that is required for "
"execution and post-processing. An important practical advantage is that both"
" the template circuit and the samplex are created locally on the client "
"side. As a result, you can inspect, validate, and debug them before you use "
"any hardware resources.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Samplomatic has many benefits, including fine-grained control of adaptive "
"shot allocation and randomization. It also has logistical benefits, such as "
"structured post-processing workflows. One of the most important benefits is "
"the reuse of learned noise information across identical layers, which can "
"save a significant amount of quantum computing time during noise learning.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shaded lightcones {#shaded-lightcones}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Before you decide where to apply expensive mitigation techniques, it is "
"helpful to identify the regions of a circuit that matter most to the "
"observable that is being measured. This need motivates the [shaded "
"lightcones](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-addon-slc) addon. The"
" idea of a lightcone comes from relativity, in which causal relationships "
"are constrained by the finite speed at which information can travel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![An image of spacetime. Time runs from bottom to top, and space runs from "
"side to side. Because no causation can move through space faster than the "
"speed of light, the region of spacetime that an event can causally influence"
" is limited to a conical region. This is an analogy for the spreading of "
"errors in a quantum circuit.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-"
"advantage/modern-workflows/lightcone.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A similar concept applies in quantum circuits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Consider a large circuit in which only a low-weight observable is of "
"interest. A low-weight observable acts nontrivially on only a few qubits. "
"Low-weight observables are of interest for many reasons. For example, they "
"can represent the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Individual terms within a larger Hamiltonian\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A localized interaction region\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Information collected onto a small set of ancilla qubits\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Other observables that involve only a few qubits\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Not every error in the circuit can affect the measurement outcome. This is "
"especially true of Pauli errors, or any error that can be modeled as a set "
"of Pauli operations. For example, look at the following circuit:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A quantum circuit with an error on qubit 0 just before measurement. A "
"measurement is performed only on qubit 6, and there is no entangling gate "
"between qubits 0 and 6 in the final layer.](/learning/images/courses/tools-"
"for-quantum-advantage/modern-workflows/lightcone-a.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A Pauli error on qubit 0 in the final layer cannot influence the measurement"
" on qubit 6, because there is no connecting gate that could propagate the "
"error from qubit 0 to qubit 6. Note that this argument does not apply to "
"certain non-unitary errors. For example, a T1-like transition to the ground "
"state on qubit 0 collapses qubit 0 to a computational basis state. If qubits"
" 0 and 6 were previously entangled (as they are in this circuit), the non-"
"unitary error on qubit 0 can affect qubit 6. The following discussion "
"focuses on errors that can be represented as Pauli operations, or as "
"probabilistic mixtures of Pauli operations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the last layer, only a Pauli error on qubit 5 or qubit 6 can directly "
"affect the measurement on qubit 6.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the entangling layer before that one, qubits 5 and 6 are entangled with "
"qubits 4 and 7, respectively. Therefore, in that entangling layer or just "
"before it, a Pauli error on any of those four qubits can affect the later "
"measurement. You can continue to trace backward through the circuit to find "
"the subset of gates and qubits that are causally connected to the "
"measurement. This causal set forms a cone-shaped region in space (qubits) "
"and time (layers), which gives rise to the term lightcone. The exact shape "
"of the region varies from circuit to circuit. Because this analysis depends "
"on hardware connectivity and gate placement, it is most useful after "
"transpilation, routing, and layout optimization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Beyond causal connectivity {#beyond-causal-connectivity}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Shaded lightcones extend this idea by also considering which types of errors"
" matter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For example, note the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A Z error immediately before a Z-basis measurement has no effect on the "
"measurement outcome.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An X error immediately before a Z-basis measurement can dramatically change "
"the result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Some error types therefore deserve more mitigation effort than others. This "
"distinction can substantially reduce the overhead of techniques such as PEC."
" In practice, the addon generates separate sensitivity analyses for each of "
"the following error types:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "X errors\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Z errors\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Y (or XZ up to a phase) errors\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This error sensitivity generally looks different for each error type, "
"because different causally connected errors have different impacts on the "
"measurement. Note also that the sensitivity changes throughout the circuit. "
"An X error immediately before measurement might be highly damaging, while an"
" error that occurs many layers earlier might have a smaller effect after it "
"propagates through a highly entangled state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Three images showing the same quantum circuit, but with (a) no error, (b) "
"an error just before measurement, and (c) an error earlier in the "
"circuit.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/modern-"
"workflows/sensitivity.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Shaded lightcones quantify this difference by assigning a sensitivity value "
"to potential errors throughout the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The resulting maps indicate the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Where errors matter\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Which error types matter\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "How strongly they influence the final observable\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Three images showing heat maps of error sensitivity to three different "
"error types: X, Y, and Z.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-"
"advantage/modern-workflows/slc-xyz.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Shaded lightcones do not mitigate errors themselves, but they can help focus"
" mitigation effort where it provides the greatest benefit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Learn and inject noise {#learn-and-inject-noise}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"After a circuit has been constructed, optimized, and annotated, two "
"complementary tasks remain: learning the noise, and using the noise model "
"(for example, to inject noise or to propagate the effects of noise).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "NoiseLearnerV3 {#noiselearnerv3}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"NoiseLearnerV3 is the current tool for noise learning. The previous "
"NoiseLearner interface accepted entire circuits and produced Pauli-Lindblad "
"noise models. NoiseLearnerV3 instead operates directly on boxed circuit "
"instructions. In summary, NoiseLearnerV3 does the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Accepts collections of labeled circuit layers\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reuses information from repeated layers\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Supports measurement-noise learning\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Supports post-processing of learned models\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Enables noise injection and scaling workflows\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This design aligns closely with the layer-oriented workflow of Samplomatic. "
"In particular, NoiseLearnerV3 uses the following methods for each "
"instruction set:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli-Lindblad learning for gate operations\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "TREX-based learning for measurements\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can then use a learned noise model in many of the methods that we have "
"already covered. For example, recall that zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) "
"with gate folding can lead to intractably deep circuits. The alternative "
"method for scaling noise in that case was to sample from a known noise "
"model, which effectively increases the noise level, even by non-integer "
"factors. This method, probabilistic error amplification (PEA), is a good "
"example of how you can use a noise model to mitigate errors. A good noise "
"model is also required for probabilistic error cancellation (PEC) and "
"propagated noise absorption (PNA).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In short, with a high-quality noise model, noise becomes a programmable "
"resource rather than only a source of error.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Shaded lightcones versus NoiseLearnerV3 {#shaded-lightcones-versus-"
"noiselearnerv3}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"NoiseLearnerV3 and SLC are closely related, but they take distinct inputs "
"and answer different questions. SLC determines where to focus mitigation "
"effort. NoiseLearnerV3 characterizes the actual noise present in those "
"regions. These processes also involve different kinds of overhead. The "
"following table summarizes these differences.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"|   | Shaded lightcones | NoiseLearnerV3 |\n"
"|---|-------------------|----------------|\n"
"| Purpose | Identifies where mitigation matters | Learns how errors behave |\n"
"| Overhead | Primarily classical overhead | Requires hardware executions |\n"
"| Output | Produces sensitivity maps | Produces noise-channel descriptions |\n"
"| Input | Uses circuit structure and operator propagation | Uses circuit structure and experimental hardware data |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Executor and runtime execution {#executor-and-runtime-execution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Executor performs the final execution step. It can operate on either of the "
"following inputs:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A template circuit and samplex generated by Samplomatic\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A conventional quantum circuit without Samplomatic\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"With this flexibility, you can adopt as many or as few components of the "
"directed execution workflow as you need.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Executor works closely with Samplomatic, which manages randomization "
"choices, shot allocation, noise-injection selections, and related execution "
"metadata, as described previously.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For practical examples of the directed execution model, see the [Directed "
"execution model](/guides/directed-execution-model) guide as well as the "
"[tutorials](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/tutorials) and [Qiskit addon "
"documentation](/guides/addons) on [IBM Quantum&reg; "
"Platform](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Conclusion {#conclusion}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Modern quantum workflows increasingly rely on a collection of complementary "
"tools rather than a single execution primitive.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These tools support every stage of the workflow:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Problem mapping\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Circuit construction\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Transpilation and optimization\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Error suppression\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Error mitigation\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Noise learning\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Structured execution\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Post-processing\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Together, tools such as Samplomatic, Executor, NoiseLearnerV3, shaded "
"lightcones, propagated noise absorption, circuit cutting, and AQC-Tensor "
"provide significantly more control than earlier execution models.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The field continues to evolve rapidly. As quantum processors become larger "
"and more connected, these techniques are likely to play an increasingly "
"important role in achieving useful quantum advantage, and eventually in "
"integrating seamlessly with fault-tolerant quantum computing workflows.\n"
msgstr ""
